---
// Lesson 7.5 — Design a Big Cave: Flow & Difficulty
// Level design theory: sightlines, pacing, difficulty curves, signposting.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.5 — Design a Big Cave: Flow &amp; Difficulty</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have all the tools — tiles, enemies, ladders, spikes, turrets.
    Tools do not make a level; <strong>flow</strong> does. Today, zero
    code: you learn to read a level like a designer, sketch your whole
    big cave on paper, and only then paint it.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Concept Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>1. Flow: the path is the message.</strong> Players move
    toward: open space, light, coins, and AWAY from spikes and
    enemies. A level steers without a single word.</p>
    <pre><code>Good: room → glimpse of next room through a gap → curiosity pulls
Bad:  room → identical exits left/right both dark → player stops,
      and a stopped player reads the room as a wall</code></pre>

    <p><strong>2. Pacing: the beat chart.</strong> Any good cave is a
    rhythm of TENSION (threat) and RELEASE (safety + reward):</p>
    <table>
        <tr><td>Section</td><td>Beat</td><td>Feel</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1 Entrance</td><td>quiet</td><td>"I belong here"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 Teeth</td><td>tension</td><td>spike jumps — first death possible</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 Ladder + turret</td><td>release → tension</td><td>climb while shot at</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 Rest + coins</td><td>release</td><td>breath, spend coins mentally</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5 Gauntlet</td><td>peak tension</td><td>everything at once</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6 Boss door</td><td>quiet</td><td>the door says "next time"</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>3. Difficulty curve (again, at scale).</strong> The
    personality-turret ramp from <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a> was
    three copies of a knob; this is the same idea with rooms. Rule of
    thumb: each section may introduce ONE new combination (spikes +
    turret = one new thing; not spikes + turret + ladder + moving
    platform = four).</p>

    <p><strong>4. Signposting: telegraph everything.</strong> Safe
    routes look safe. Danger is visible BEFORE it is threatening
    (the turret is seen at rest before it shoots). The exit is
    visible from the entrance (or glimpsed once). Your soft gate
    from <a href="6.4.html">6.4</a> is signposting for the
    boots.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Difficulty and fairness are different axes: hard-but-fair
            vs easy-but-random. Using sightlines and telegraphs,
            explain how your cave can be the FIRST while your
            random-placement impulse (we all have it) makes the
            SECOND. Which one do testers call "good" even when they
            die?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Design Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the paper sketch.</strong> Pencil, one page,
    your whole cave: all 6 beats as rooms/boxes, with arrows for the
    path and notes for threats. No detail on any single screen —
    flow first. This paper IS the design; the tilemap is just a
    transcription of it.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the camera question.</strong> Your game camera
    shows a fixed width. Mark on your sketch what is VISIBLE at each
    beat: what does the player see from section 2? They should see
    the goal of section 2 and the PROMISE of section 3 (a glimpse,
    a light, a coin trail). Adjust boxes so each screen shows: this
    beat's challenge + a hint of the next one.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — the death audit.</strong> Walk your sketch
    and ask at every beat: if the player dies HERE, what do they
    lose? Under 20 seconds of progress = healthy. Over 60 = spite
    (either add a checkpoint trigger, or shorten the section — your
    <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> respawn loop is all you get unless
    you build checkpoints, which you may).</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Design smell test</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Sketch audit: is there ANY beat that introduces two
                new things at once? Which thing moves to a later
                beat?</li>
            <li>Stand (mentally) in beat 4 (rest) — can the player
                see a spike from there? Why must rest beats be
                visually clean of threats?</li>
            <li>Count screens between the boss door and the last
                save/heal. Is your potion before or after the peak
                tension — and why do great games heal BEFORE the
                boss, not after?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Trade sketches</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Trade sketches with a partner. They point at any box:
                you must say the BEAT and the FEEL sentence in
                five seconds. If you hesitate, the sketch is not
                clear enough yet.</li>
            <li>Partner marks your path with a pencil "blind" — where
                do THEY think the player will go? Anywhere their
                line diverges from your intended path is a
                signposting bug found for free.</li>
            <li>Commit + push a photo/scan of the final sketch:
                <code>"7.5a: cave sketch + beat chart"</code>. The
                sketch goes in the repo — design docs are code.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain flow, pacing, difficulty curve, signposting — each in one sentence</li>
            <li>Draw a 6-beat cave sketch with path arrows and threat notes</li>
            <li>Run a camera-visibility pass and a death audit on your sketch</li>
            <li>Explain hard-but-fair vs easy-but-random with examples from your own sketch</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the design theory page</li>
        <li><a href="../data/playtests.csv">Playtest data (CSV)</a> — the numbers that argue about difficulty</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.6.html">7.6 — Level Studio I</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="7.5" title="7.5a Design a Big Cave: Flow &amp; Difficulty — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
